{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '205,340p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '475,515p'; tail -20 docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md; rg -n 'declared|:decl|macro-defined|admission|literal|batch-local' docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md\"","aggregated_output":"   205\t :eval     (fn [lambda-ast env prelude budget] → value)   ; §3.3\n   206\t :t        0}                     ; batch counter; incremented once per expand-batch\n   207\t```\n   208\t\n   209\t`expand-batch`:\n   210\t\n   211\t1. **Admit.** The batch is indexed with `index-datoms` and checked as a\n   212\t   whole — **every entity in the index, not only those reachable from the\n   213\t   root**, because harvest (step 2) reads disconnected definitions: every\n   214\t   `:yin/type` is in the closed vocabulary (§3.3), every ref resolves to an\n   215\t   entity in the index, the whole graph is acyclic, every ground value is\n   216\t   plain data, and every `:macro? true` lambda is the value operand of a\n   217\t   `(yin/def <literal sym> …)` — a macro lambda anywhere else has no meaning\n   218\t   on either side of the boundary and, forwarded, would load on the walker\n   219\t   as a plain closure, the quiet case decision 5 exists to prevent — all\n   220\t   *before* any recursive decoding (Appendix B). An in-memory medium can\n   221\t   carry host values, and the sandbox's closed-input guarantee holds only if\n   222\t   nothing else is admitted. A failure here is\n   223\t   `{:status :error :error {:kind :malformed-input :reason :unknown-type |\n   224\t   :dangling-ref | :cyclic | :host-value | :stray-macro-lambda :eid e}}`\n   225\t   carrying a **batch-level\n   226\t   event** (§4.1) — allocated from the watermark alone, since the index may\n   227\t   be unusable — and the advanced `:ctx`. Diagnostics name entities, never\n   228\t   carry the offending values.\n   229\t2. **Harvest.** `definitions` finds every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` in the\n   230\t   batch, in batch order, last wins: `v` a `:macro? true` lambda →\n   231\t   `assoc` into `:store`; `v` anything else (a plain lambda, a variable, a\n   232\t   literal) → `dissoc`, so a redefinition as a non-macro stops expansion of\n   233\t   that name. Harvest is *syntactic* and whole-batch by policy: a definition\n   234\t   in an unexecuted branch, or after its first use in the same batch, is\n   235\t   still in force for the whole batch. (Clojure defines a macro only when the\n   236\t   `defmacro` form executes; this design does not, and says so.)\n   237\t3. **Replace.** Step 2 also numbers every source macro definition it saw,\n   238\t   in harvest order, into a batch-local **catalogue** `:declared {k {:name\n   239\t   sym :lambda ast}}`. Each macro definition node is then replaced by a\n   240\t   **stand-in** `{:type :yin/macro-defined :name <sym> :decl k}` — an\n   241\t   internal node type of the expander's working representation, not of any\n   242\t   evaluator. It carries its identity by *shape and ordinal*, so it survives\n   243\t   every transformation a macro can apply to it: a macro that receives it\n   244\t   as an operand and returns, wraps, or reorders it hands back the same\n   245\t   shape, and nothing in the pipeline needs an eid or `m` to recognise it —\n   246\t   and two definitions of the same name in one batch remain distinguishable,\n   247\t   so a macro that keeps one and discards the other keeps *that one's* body.\n   248\t   A fabricated stand-in can only name a catalogue entry (step 5 validates\n   249\t   `k` and that `:name` matches); it can therefore move a harvested\n   250\t   declaration's effective position — re-asserting it after a plain\n   251\t   definition, say — but never introduce an unharvested body. That is\n   252\t   permitted transformer behaviour and is stated as such. Step 7 lowers\n   253\t   every stand-in to `{:type :literal :value <sym>}` for `program-out`, so a\n   254\t   batch whose root is a definition still forwards a complete program and a\n   255\t   REPL prints the name. Plain `yin/def`s are forwarded untouched.\n   256\t4. **Expand.** `expand-node` (§3.2) runs from the root over the index.\n   257\t5. **Post-harvest.** Expansion can *form* definitions that did not have the\n   258\t   `yin/def` shape in the source — `(defn m …)` above all, but also an\n   259\t   operator that rewrites into `yin/def` — and copying along changed paths\n   260\t   gives nodes fresh eids and `default-op`, so neither eid nor `m` can say\n   261\t   where a definition came from. Step 5 therefore does not ask. The store\n   262\t   for the **next** batch is recomputed as: start from the store *as it was\n   263\t   before step 2*; walk the **final tree** in **declaration order** (below);\n   264\t   at every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` occurrence, a macro lambda `v`\n   265\t   `assoc`es and anything else `dissoc`es; at every `:yin/macro-defined`\n   266\t   stand-in, look up `:decl k` in this batch's catalogue and, if the entry\n   267\t   exists and its `:name` equals the stand-in's, `assoc` *that entry's*\n   268\t   lambda (any other stand-in — unknown `k`, mismatched name — is ignored).\n   269\t   Last occurrence in declaration order wins. Position is a\n   270\t   property of the tree, not of the node, so re-parenting cannot lose it;\n   271\t   a definition formed by rewriting is present in the tree like any other;\n   272\t   and a stand-in is recognised by shape, so passing through a macro cannot\n   273\t   lose it either.\n   274\t\n   275\t   **Declaration order** is *not* the expansion traversal order of §3.2\n   276\t   (which is operator-first so that fresh ids are deterministic). yang\n   277\t   lowers `(do A B)` to `((fn [_] B) A)` and `(let [x v] body)` to\n   278\t   `((fn [x] body) v)`; operator-first would visit B before A. Declaration\n   279\t   order is: for an `:application` whose operator is a `:lambda`, the\n   280\t   operands left to right, then the lambda's body; for every other node,\n   281\t   its children in the fixed order. This recovers source sequence order for\n   282\t   every `do`/`let` nesting yang.clojure produces and for the Python/PHP\n   283\t   suite lowerings, which use the same immediately-applied-lambda shape. It\n   284\t   is deterministic, but *not* a universal source-order guarantee: a\n   285\t   frontend construct lowered through a non-lambda operator (PHP's `for`,\n   286\t   whose loop operator is a fixpoint) ranks operator syntax before outer\n   287\t   operands. The claim made is the narrow one. **Ordering rule:** step 2's\n   288\t   source-only store governs the whole current batch; step 5's result\n   289\t   governs from the next batch. Consequences, stated: `(def m f)` then\n   290\t   `(defmacro m …)` keeps the macro (its stand-in is later in declaration\n   291\t   order); `(identity-macro (defmacro m …))` keeps it (the stand-in comes\n   292\t   back by shape); `(keep-first (defmacro m … :A) (defmacro m … :B))` keeps\n   293\t   **A** for the next batch — the surviving stand-in's `:decl` selects A's\n   294\t   body, although B won step 2 for the current batch;\n   295\t   `(defmacro m …)` then `(defn m …)` or `((choose-def) 'm f)` removes it\n   296\t   (the formed plain definition is later); a source macro definition that\n   297\t   an enclosing macro *discards* from its output is in force for the current\n   298\t   batch (step 2) and absent from the next (not in the final tree) — the\n   299\t   final tree is the program; within one batch a macro `m` and a `(defn m\n   300\t   …)` both apply to their own forms in source order, as Clojure would not.\n   301\t   The design accepts these one-batch divergences in exchange for a\n   302\t   one-pass, deterministic rule that needs no origin tracking.\n   303\t6. **Mark tail.** `mark-tail` recomputes `:tail?` over the whole final tree\n   304\t   (§3.4).\n   305\t7. **Emit.** Every `:yin/macro-defined` stand-in is lowered to\n   306\t   `{:type :literal :value <sym>}`; output is then emitted bottom-up as a\n   307\t   self-contained batch with a fresh `:yin/root` fact; the log copy carries\n   308\t   `m` per §4.1. No evaluator ever sees the internal node type.\n   309\t8. **Scan.** A final walk asserts no lambda with `:yin/macro? true` remains.\n   310\t   With admission rejecting stray macro lambdas, step 3 replacing source\n   311\t   definitions, and `:generated-macro` rejecting generated ones, this cannot\n   312\t   fire; it is the guard for decision 5 and a violation is a forwarder\n   313\t   defect, thrown as `{:kind :scan-failed :eid e}`.\n   314\t\n   315\tAn `:error` result carries the event (§4.1) in `:log` and the advanced\n   316\t`:ctx`; `:datoms` is absent. `expand` unwraps `:ok` and throws on `:error`\n   317\tfor callers that want the exception. Same inputs, same output, on every\n   318\thost.\n   319\t\n   320\t**Working representation.** Traversal, cycle detection, and shadow tracking\n   321\trun over *eids in the index*. Plain AST maps are materialised only at the\n   322\tboundary of one body invocation — operands decoded for `invoke`, the returned\n   323\tvalue validated and re-encoded to datoms with fresh ids, and re-expansion\n   324\tcontinues over the index of the batch-so-far. Internal identity never\n   325\tappears in the map representation macros see (`valid-ast?` rejects `:eid`).\n   326\t\n   327\t**Allocation.** `:alloc` is seeded per batch to\n   328\t`(min (:next-eid alloc) (dec min-eid-in-batch) (- (inc datom/first-user-id)))`\n   329\t— the incoming watermark is a term, so the counter only ever decreases across\n   330\ta session and event eids on the accumulated log never collide. It is returned\n   331\tadvanced in both `:ok` and `:error` results; a retry of a staged flush reuses\n   332\tthe staged allocation and never allocates again.\n   333\t\n   334\t### 3.2 The algorithm\n   335\t\n   336\tTraversal visits ref attributes in a **fixed order** so fresh ids and\n   337\tgensyms are deterministic across hosts: `:yin/operator`, `:yin/operands`\n   338\t(left to right), `:yin/test`, `:yin/consequent`, `:yin/alternate`,\n   339\t`:yin/body`, `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node`, `:yin/source`. This order is\n   340\tfor allocation determinism only; it is not source order (§3.1 step 5's\n   475\t\"Run a lambda under a step budget in a fresh VM\" is the one capability the\n   476\texpander needs that `yin.vm` does not export today. It is a general\n   477\tfacility — any process that runs another's code wants it — and is the\n   478\treason the expander can later become a `yin.vm` program itself (§7,\n   479\tReserved) without any macro-specific primitive.\n   480\t\n   481\t**Validation.** The same `valid-ast?` runs on what goes *in* (every operand\n   482\tAST decoded for a body, and every ground value at admission) and on what\n   483\tcomes *out* (the body's value): a closed, plain-data Universal AST. It\n   484\tenforces, per node, exactly the vocabulary `ast->datoms-with-root` emits:\n   485\t\n   486\t| `:type` | Required | Optional |\n   487\t|---|---|---|\n   488\t| `:literal` | `:value` plain data | `:tail?` |\n   489\t| `:yin/macro-defined` | `:name` symbol, `:decl` int | — (expander-internal, §3.1 step 3; accepted in operands and output, never emitted) |\n   490\t| `:variable` | `:name` symbol | `:tail?` |\n   491\t| `:lambda` | `:params` vector of symbols, `:body` | `:tail?` `:macro?` |\n   492\t| `:application` | `:operator`, `:operands` vector | `:tail?` |\n   493\t| `:if` | `:test` `:consequent` `:alternate` | `:tail?` |\n   494\t| `:dao.stream.apply/call` | `:op` keyword, `:operands` | `:tail?` |\n   495\t| `:vm/gensym` | | `:prefix` string |\n   496\t| `:vm/store-get` | `:key` | |\n   497\t| `:vm/store-put` | `:key` `:val` plain data | |\n   498\t| `:vm/park`, `:vm/current-continuation` | | |\n   499\t| `:vm/resume` | `:parked-id` keyword, `:val` | |\n   500\t| `:stream/make` | | `:buffer` int |\n   501\t| `:stream/put` | `:target` `:val` | |\n   502\t| `:stream/cursor`, `:stream/next`, `:stream/close` | `:source` | |\n   503\t\n   504\tAny other `:type`, any key outside the row, any `:eid`, a `:macro? true`\n   505\tlambda where `:allow-macro-lambda?` is false, or any non-plain value\n   506\tanywhere (recursively through vectors, maps, and sets, including inside\n   507\t`:literal :value`) is `{:kind :invalid-output :macro eid :at [path]}` (or\n   508\t`:malformed-input` at admission).\n   509\t*Plain data* is nil, booleans, numbers, strings, keywords, symbols, and\n   510\tvectors/maps/sets thereof. `:vm/store-update` is absent: it is a walker-only\n   511\ttransition with no datom form. The walk also counts nodes for the\n   512\tper-expansion bound, checked *before* emission so oversized output never\n   513\tmaterialises.\n   514\t\n   515\t**The prelude** — primitives merged into the throwaway VM for one body:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 31 | `m` is emission provenance, not definition origin: a re-parented generated definition carries `default-op`, and an operator rewrite forms a `yin/def` under `default-op`; both escape post-harvest (astra P2) | §3.1 step 5 ranks every `yin/def` occurrence in the *final tree* in traversal order, with step 3's recorded stand-in literals (`:replaced`) representing source macro definitions; no origin tracking, no use of eid or `m`; discarded-definition consequence stated; both examples in Phase 1 |\n| 32 | `macro-of`'s inline macro-lambda arm is unreachable after admission (astra P3) | §2.3 drops the inline case as supported input; §3.2 marks the arm defensive |\n\nRound 6 (`collab/1789280492428-review-macro-stream-process-r6.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`), on revision 6:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 33 | Operator-first traversal visits `((fn [_] B) A)` as B then A, inverting `do`/`let` declaration precedence (astra P2-1) | §3.1 step 5 defines **declaration order** separately from expansion traversal: immediately-applied lambdas rank operands before body; §3.2 notes its order is for allocation only; Phase 1 tests run through real lowering with nested sequences |\n| 34 | Stand-in literal loses its recorded eid when a macro decodes and re-encodes it (astra P2-2) | Step 3 stand-in is an internal node type `:yin/macro-defined` recognised by shape; `:replaced` removed; validator accepts it on both sides; step 7 lowers it to a literal for `program-out`; fabricated stand-ins are inert; identity/wrapper/discard/plain-literal cases in Phase 1 |\n\nRound 7 (`collab/1789280741657-review-macro-stream-process-r7.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`), on revision 7:\n\n| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| 35 | Name-only stand-ins cannot distinguish two same-name source definitions; a macro keeping the first reinstalls the second (astra P2) | Step 2 numbers definitions into a batch-local catalogue; stand-in carries `:decl k`; step 5 resolves `k` and checks the name; keep-first/keep-second/swap/duplicate cases in Phase 1 |\n| 36 | \"Fabrication gains nothing\" too broad — a valid fabricated stand-in moves a declaration's precedence (astra) | Step 3 states it: may move, never introduce a body; Phase 1 test |\n| 37 | Declaration order is not a universal source-order guarantee (PHP `for`'s fixpoint operator) (astra) | Step 5 narrows the claim to `do`/`let` and the Python/PHP suite shape, and names the exception |\n108:| No assumed graphs | The expander indexes each batch with `index-datoms` explicitly, checks the reachable graph acyclic and every ref resolvable at admission, visits ref attributes in a fixed order, and honours the explicit `:yin/root` fact. Malformed input, dangling roots, and forged ids are errors naming the entity. |\n134:`[{:type :literal :value <sym>} <lambda macro? true>]` — the shape\n152:admission rejects a macro lambda anywhere but a definition's value operand,\n159:may arrive as a `:variable`, a `:literal` symbol, or a `:literal` string.\n217:   `(yin/def <literal sym> …)` — a macro lambda anywhere else has no meaning\n229:2. **Harvest.** `definitions` finds every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` in the\n232:   literal) → `dissoc`, so a redefinition as a non-macro stops expansion of\n238:   in harvest order, into a batch-local **catalogue** `:declared {k {:name\n240:   **stand-in** `{:type :yin/macro-defined :name <sym> :decl k}` — an\n253:   every stand-in to `{:type :literal :value <sym>}` for `program-out`, so a\n264:   at every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` occurrence, a macro lambda `v`\n265:   `assoc`es and anything else `dissoc`es; at every `:yin/macro-defined`\n266:   stand-in, look up `:decl k` in this batch's catalogue and, if the entry\n293:   **A** for the next batch — the surviving stand-in's `:decl` selects A's\n305:7. **Emit.** Every `:yin/macro-defined` stand-in is lowered to\n306:   `{:type :literal :value <sym>}`; output is then emitted bottom-up as a\n310:   With admission rejecting stray macro lambdas, step 3 replacing source\n348:    :lambda  l   → l when (:macro? l)     ; defensive only: unreachable after admission (§3.1) and :generated-macro\n376:dangling-ref detection happened at admission (§3.1 step 1), so this walk is\n417:  false` on expansion output. With admission also rejecting source macro\n469:traversal of one large literal; `:max-nodes-per-expansion` bounds AST nodes,\n470:not literal payload size. Both are known limits of v1, recorded here rather\n472:payload bound at admission (§3.1 step 1), which is where such a bound\n482:AST decoded for a body, and every ground value at admission) and on what\n488:| `:literal` | `:value` plain data | `:tail?` |\n489:| `:yin/macro-defined` | `:name` symbol, `:decl` int | — (expander-internal, §3.1 step 3; accepted in operands and output, never emitted) |\n507:`:literal :value`) is `{:kind :invalid-output :macro eid :at [path]}` (or\n508:`:malformed-input` at admission).\n520:| `yin/literal v`, `yin/variable sym` | leaf constructors |\n522:| `yin/sequence-body [ast ...]` | the `do` desugaring yang uses; empty → `nil` literal |\n523:| `yin/make-lambda params-ast body-ast` | `params-ast` is a `:literal` whose value is the param vector |\n524:| `yin/make-def name-ast value-ast` | `(yin/def <sym> value)`; `name-ast` may be `:variable`, `:literal` symbol, or `:literal` string |\n583:| cyclic / dangling / host-valued input | admission | — | `{:kind :malformed-input :reason :cyclic|:dangling-ref|:host-value :eid}` |\n596:rejected at admission. Events and the `m`-tagged output are appended to the\n603:[ev :yin/source-call    <call-eid>           t default-op]   ; eid in that batch, or in the log copy of the enclosing expansion; absent on an admission failure\n604:[ev :yin/macro-name     <sym>                t default-op]   ; when the operator was a :variable; absent for an inline macro lambda or an admission failure\n611:An **admission-failure event** is the same entity with only `:yin/type`,\n628:log to `dao.space`; the transactor relocates only declared refs, so a\n798:`defmacro` surface and is reserved; the Python tokenizer has no list-literal\n855:native lowering; definition consumed and replaced by a name literal; a\n873:one yields that one's; an ordinary `'m` literal declares nothing; a\n874:fabricated stand-in with an unknown `:decl`, or a known `:decl` with a\n876:`:decl` placed after a plain `yin/def m` re-asserts that declaration (the\n877:stated permitted behaviour); no `:yin/macro-defined` reaches\n879:admission as `:stray-macro-lambda`;\n891:host value in a literal, and a cycle inside a disconnected definition all\n892:fail at admission before any decode, each producing one admission event\n894:literal and an `:eid` key; arity and `&`; **gensyms differ across two\n929:[(yin/literal 1)])]))` called as `twice(inc_fn)` from Python and\n933:semantic. Python `defn` stays reserved until the parser has list literals.\n941:payload-size bounds at admission for untrusted authors; Shibi at the medium\n1058:| 11 | Output validation alone does not close the sandbox input (astra P2-9) | §3.1 step 1 admission validates ground values; §3.3 validates operands going in; `:eval`/prelude trust contract stated; fuel and payload limits recorded as known v1 limits |\n1059:| 12 | Cycle detection after recursive decode overflows; `eid(node)` undefined on maps (astra P2-10, glm P3-8) | §3.1 admission over the index; working-representation paragraph; Appendix B |\n1077:| 23 | Admission failures have no event path (astra P2-6) | §4.1 admission-failure event allocated from the watermark, no input values in diagnostics |\n1088:| 29 | Stray source macro lambda outside a definition shape has no disposition (glm P3-1) | §3.1 step 1 `:stray-macro-lambda` at admission; step 8 named `:scan-failed` as a forwarder defect |\n1095:| 31 | `m` is emission provenance, not definition origin: a re-parented generated definition carries `default-op`, and an operator rewrite forms a `yin/def` under `default-op`; both escape post-harvest (astra P2) | §3.1 step 5 ranks every `yin/def` occurrence in the *final tree* in traversal order, with step 3's recorded stand-in literals (`:replaced`) representing source macro definitions; no origin tracking, no use of eid or `m`; discarded-definition consequence stated; both examples in Phase 1 |\n1096:| 32 | `macro-of`'s inline macro-lambda arm is unreachable after admission (astra P3) | §2.3 drops the inline case as supported input; §3.2 marks the arm defensive |\n1103:| 34 | Stand-in literal loses its recorded eid when a macro decodes and re-encodes it (astra P2-2) | Step 3 stand-in is an internal node type `:yin/macro-defined` recognised by shape; `:replaced` removed; validator accepts it on both sides; step 7 lowers it to a literal for `program-out`; fabricated stand-ins are inert; identity/wrapper/discard/plain-literal cases in Phase 1 |\n1109:| 35 | Name-only stand-ins cannot distinguish two same-name source definitions; a macro keeping the first reinstalls the second (astra P2) | Step 2 numbers definitions into a batch-local catalogue; stand-in carries `:decl k`; step 5 resolves `k` and checks the name; keep-first/keep-second/swap/duplicate cases in Phase 1 |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-13 06:31:53 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-13 13:31:53 +07\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a098f8-8420-7083-9d18-f9776512bd61\nRole: Routine Review | Model: gpt-6-astra\n\n**Round-7 P2: Resolved.** [§3.1’s catalogue and ordinal lookup](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:237) distinguish same-name source definitions and restore the lambda selected by the surviving stand-in.\n\nBoth probe items are adequately stated: valid fabrication may change precedence without introducing an unharvested body, and declaration order makes only the narrow frontend-order guarantee.\n\nNo files were written. These are in-memory reductions of the specified rule, not implementation tests.\n\n**Reductions**\n\nCatalogue: `0 → {name m, lambda A}`, `1 → {name m, lambda B}`. The initial next-batch store is empty.\n\n| Returned declaration order | Next binding | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Keep first: `[0]` | A | Pass |\n| Keep second: `[1]` | B | Pass |\n| Swap: `[1, 0]` | A | Pass |\n| Duplicate first: `[0, 0]` | A | Pass |\n| Duplicate second: `[1, 1]` | B | Pass |\n| Unknown ordinal | None | Ignored |\n| Known ordinal, mismatched name | None | Ignored |\n| Plain definition, then fabricated valid `0` | A | Permitted reassertion |\n| Change received `:decl 0` to `:decl 1` | B | Permitted selection |\n\nChanging `:decl` to another same-name entry is acceptable under the stated contract. Both bodies were harvested from this batch; the transformer changes which admitted declaration survives. The catalogue, rather than the returned marker, supplies the body.\n\n**Stale-ordinal probe**\n\nBatch-local lookup prevents resurrection of a previous batch’s declaration body. It does **not** establish that an old marker value can never be replayed.\n\nA marker is plain data: equivalent data can be retained externally, embedded in literal syntax, or fabricated later. The reductions show:\n\n| Replayed marker | Current catalogue | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| `{name m, decl 0}` | Empty | Ignored |\n| `{name m, decl 0}` | `0 → {name m, lambda C}` | Selects current-batch C |\n| `{name m, decl 0}` | `0 → {name n, lambda C}` | Ignored |\n\nThis is consistent with permitted fabrication. It cannot select the old body through an old catalogue.\n\n**[P3 — suggestion/alignment] Clarify catalogue lifetime versus marker lifetime.**\n\nDescribe the guarantee as: “Stand-ins resolve exclusively against the current batch’s fresh catalogue; they carry no cross-batch declaration identity.” Avoid claiming that marker-shaped data cannot survive outside one expansion.\n\nNo batch nonce is required under the chosen fabrication semantics. A nonce would be necessary only for a stronger stale-marker rejection guarantee that this design does not currently promise.\n\nNo blocking design findings remain in this confirmation. Approval remains subject to implementing and validating the documented Phase 0 prerequisites and acceptance tests.\n\n**Verdict: APPROVE.**"}}
